<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Peer sync talks plain HTTP to other devices on the local network, and to a
    self-hosted PocketBase reached over Tailscale. Android blocks cleartext by
    default from targetSdk 28, which silently kills every outgoing sync — the
    app can be connected *to*, but can never connect *out*.

    Android's network security config matches domains and literal hosts, not CIDR
    ranges, so there is no way to say "private addresses only" here. Cleartext is
    therefore permitted generally, which is acceptable because:

      - peer traffic never leaves the local network,
      - nothing moves until two devices have paired and exchanged a secret,
      - every sync request carries that secret, and
      - the server address is one the user typed themselves.

    If this ever talks to something on the public internet, it should be HTTPS
    and this file should be narrowed to the specific hosts that need cleartext.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
